    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_0f394672400850a7c847731b5511c8812d25e10d566954e903616d90701d6fbe",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "Because pi*S(t) is the argument variation along a horizontal path from the right to the critical line, the ordered crossings of real- and imaginary-axis preimages geometrically accumulate that discrepancy. Crossing complexity then explains the stated |S| thresholds for failures of Gram's and Rosser's laws, while unbounded S(t) motivates the source's prediction of coils involving arbitrarily many branches.",
